course: space
lesson: 26
title: Exoplanets and the search for life
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: An exoplanet is a planet going round a star other than our Sun, and we now know thousands of them.
explain: They are far too faint to see beside their bright stars, so we catch them by tricks: when a planet passes in front of its star, the star dims a tiny bit.
explain: It is like a moth flying across a distant lighthouse: you cannot see the moth, but the light flickers.
explain: Life as we know it needs liquid water, so scientists look for planets in the "Goldilocks zone", not too hot and not too cold, like Goldilocks' porridge.
explain: So far no life has been found beyond Earth, but scientists keep looking, on far-off planets and on icy moons in our own solar system.
fact: An exoplanet is a planet that orbits a star other than the Sun, and more than 6,000 have been confirmed, a number still rising.
fact: In 1995 Michel Mayor and Didier Queloz found 51 Pegasi b, the first planet known around a Sun-like star, and won a Nobel Prize for it in 2019.
fact: Most exoplanets have been found by the transit method: a star dims slightly when a planet passes in front of it.
fact: NASA's Kepler telescope, working from 2009 to 2018, found thousands of exoplanets, and TESS has searched almost the whole sky since 2018 for more planets passing in front of their stars.
fact: Some exoplanets are "hot Jupiters", giant gas planets orbiting so close to their stars that a year lasts only a few days.
fact: The habitable or "Goldilocks" zone is the band around a star where a planet could have liquid water on its surface, not too hot and not too cold.
fact: TRAPPIST-1, a red dwarf (a small, cool, reddish star) about 40 light years away, has seven rocky planets about the size of Earth, some in its habitable zone.
fact: The James Webb Space Telescope can study the air of some exoplanets by looking at starlight that has passed through it.
fact: In our own solar system scientists look for life on Mars and in the hidden oceans under the ice of Jupiter's moon Europa and Saturn's moon Enceladus.
fact: SETI scientists listen with radio telescopes for signals from intelligent life, but no confirmed alien signal has ever been found.
fact: Some exoplanets orbit two stars, like Kepler-16b, found in 2011, a real world with two suns in its sky.
fact: No life of any kind has yet been confirmed anywhere beyond Earth.
exercise: What is an exoplanet? | A planet that orbits a star other than the Sun | A planet that has been thrown out of the solar system | A moon of Jupiter | A planet made only of ice
exercise: About how many exoplanets have been confirmed? | More than 6,000 | About 9 | Exactly 100 | More than 6 billion
exercise: What was the first planet found around a Sun-like star? | 51 Pegasi b | Kepler-16b | TRAPPIST-1 | Proxima b
exercise: When was 51 Pegasi b found? | In 1995 | In 1610 | In 1969 | In 2019
exercise: What prize did Mayor and Queloz win for their discovery? | A Nobel Prize | An Olympic medal | An Oscar | No prize at all
exercise: How have most exoplanets been found? | By a star dimming slightly as a planet passes in front | By photographing them directly with big telescopes on Earth | By sending spacecraft to them | By hearing their radio signals
exercise: Which NASA telescope found thousands of exoplanets from 2009 to 2018? | Kepler | Hubble | Voyager | Galileo
exercise: What is a hot Jupiter? | A giant gas planet orbiting very close to its star | A planet covered in volcanoes like Io | The hottest day on Jupiter | A star shaped like Jupiter
exercise: What is the habitable or Goldilocks zone? | The band round a star where liquid water could exist on a planet | The hottest part of a star | The part of a galaxy with the most stars | The zone deep inside a planet where its living things hide from the heat
exercise: What is special about TRAPPIST-1? | It has seven rocky planets about the size of Earth | It is the nearest star to the Sun | It has no planets at all | It is a giant blue star
exercise: About how far away is TRAPPIST-1? | About 40 light years | About 4 light minutes | About 40 million light years | About 4,000 light years
exercise: How can the James Webb Space Telescope study an exoplanet's air? | By looking at starlight that has passed through it | By landing a probe on the planet | By listening to its wind | By sampling the air with a scoop on a long robot arm
exercise: Which moons of our solar system may hide oceans under their ice? | Europa and Enceladus | Our Moon and Phobos | Deimos and Phobos only | None, no moon has water
exercise: What do SETI scientists do? | Listen for radio signals from intelligent life | Build rockets to fly out and visit the aliens themselves | Dig on Mars for fossils of tiny creatures in the rocks | Count all the stars in the Milky Way one by one
exercise: What is special about Kepler-16b? | It orbits two stars | It is the nearest exoplanet | It has rings like Saturn | It has life on it
test: Ruby saw a video saying "scientists found aliens on an exoplanet last week". What is true? | No life has been confirmed anywhere beyond Earth | Yes, life was found on TRAPPIST-1 | Yes, SETI got a clear message from aliens in 2019 | Yes, aliens live on Kepler-16b
test: Why can't we just take photos of most exoplanets? | They are tiny and faint beside their bright stars | They are invisible to all telescopes, even the biggest ones | They are hidden behind black holes that swallow their light | They only come out in the daytime, when the sky is too bright
test: A telescope sees a star dim slightly every 3 days. What might be happening? | A planet passes in front of it every 3 days | The star switches itself off for a short rest every 3 days | Clouds on Earth happen to pass over the telescope every 3 days | The star is moving away from us and back again
test: A planet is so close to its star that water boils away. Is it in the Goldilocks zone? | No, it is too hot | Yes, any planet near a star is | Yes, boiling water is perfect for life | No, it is too cold
test: In Star Wars, Luke watches two suns set on Tatooine. Could a real planet have two suns? | Yes, like Kepler-16b found in 2011 | No, planets can only orbit one star | Only in our own solar system | No, two suns would always crash
test: Why do scientists look under the ice of Europa and Enceladus? | Hidden oceans there might hold life | They are made of pure gold | They are the hottest moons | The ice is full of fossils
test: a hot Jupiter's year is like 4 days?? how | It orbits very close to its star, so the trip round is short | It spins very fast on its axis, so each year is only a few days | Days are longer there | Its star moves round it
test: Which is true about SETI? | It has never found a confirmed alien signal | It talks with aliens every week | It looks for life with microscopes | It found a signal in 1995 that was proved alien
test: Your friend thinks all the exoplanets were found by spacecraft flying to them. What is right? | Telescopes found them from afar, often by the dimming of stars | Yes, rockets have flown to every one of them and sent back photos | Astronauts saw them through windows | They were all found by one probe
test: If a planet sat too far from its star in the cold edge of its system, what would its water likely be? | Frozen | Boiling | Liquid like on Earth | Turned into gold
test: TESS has searched almost the whole sky since 2018. What is it looking for? | Exoplanets passing in front of their stars | Black holes in the galaxy's centre | Space junk around Earth | Comets about to hit Earth
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the term exoplanet? | A planet orbiting a star other than the Sun | A planet expelled from the solar system | A moon orbiting a giant planet | A star without any planets
exam: Ascertain the method by which the majority of exoplanets have been discovered. | The transit method, a slight dimming of the star | Direct photography of each planet with large telescopes | Visits by crewed spacecraft | The detection of artificial radio signals
exam: Identify the region around a star within which liquid water could exist on the surface of a planet. | The habitable or Goldilocks zone | The event horizon | The asteroid belt | The corona
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the search for life beyond the Earth. | No life has yet been confirmed beyond the Earth | Microbial life has been confirmed on Mars | A radio message from aliens has been confirmed by SETI | Life has been confirmed on TRAPPIST-1
